1324463,-0.0001006075763143599,-0.001032891683280468,-0.274010568857193,-0.00011860620725201443,-6.878139538457617e-05,-0.006653773132711649,-0.5958930253982544,-3.576278118089249e-07,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,-0.05777783691883087,-7.748573807475623e-06,-2.1219027985353023e-05,-2.9802276912960224e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,2132,374,2661,429,57960,839,15159,21,92,64,488,1124,839,15159,21,92,65,488,1124,839,15159,21,92,66,284,220,21,54876,1380,400,64,54876,400,65,54876,323,400,66,3,525,6785,25780,429,1352,458,7703,52484,8500,323,400,65,481,264,3,374,279,9334,315,458,7546,13,7379,400,64,488,293,488,272,12947,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIt is given that $\\log_{6}a + \\log_{6}b + \\log_{6}c = 6$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are positive integers that form an increasing geometric sequence and $b - a$ is the square of an integer. Find $a + b + c$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"9392a1687ef438ff2a997e95f0840ccd7d60dec64f75a253a125c5dfcdd6fbe7"},"seconds":63.46347093582153,"seed":465569963189652415,"stop":"eos","text":"Given the equation $\\log_{6}a + \\log_{6}b + \\log_{6}c = 6$, we can use properties of logarithms to combine the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n1. Combine the logarithms: \n\\[ \\log_{6}a + \\log_{6}b + \\log_{6}c = \\log_{6}(abc) = 6. \\]\n\n2. Convert the logarithmic equation to its exponential form:\n\\[ abc = 6^6. \\]\n\n3. Calculate $6^6$:\n\\[ 6^6 = 46656. \\]\n\nGiven that $a, b, c$ are positive integers in an increasing geometric sequence, we can express $b$ and $c$ in terms of $a$ and the common ratio $r$:\n\\[ b = ar \\quad \\text{and} \\quad c = ar^2. \\]\n\nSubstitute these into the product equation:\n\\[ a \\cdot (ar) \\cdot (ar^2) = 46656. \\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\\[ a^3 r^3 = 46656. \\]\n\nTaking the cube root of both sides:\n\\[ ar = \\sqrt[3]{46656}. \\]\n\nCalculate the cube root:\n\\[ \\sqrt[3]{46656} = 36. \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ ar = 36. \\]\n\nSince $b - a$ is a perfect square, we know:\n\\[ b - a = ar - a = a(r - 1). \\]\n\nWe need $a(r - 1)$ to be a perfect square. We also know:\n\\[ ar = 36. \\]\n\nTo find suitable values of $a$ and $r$, we list the factor pairs of 36:\n\\[ (1, 36), (2, 18), (3, 12), (4, 9), (6, 6), (9, 4), (12, 3), (18, 2), (36, 1). \\]\n\nWe check each pair to see if $a(r - 1)$ is a perfect square:\n- For $(a, r) = (1, 36)$: $a(r - 1) = 1(36 - 1) = 35$ (not a perfect square).\n- For $(a, r) = (2, 18)$: $a(r - 1) = 2(18 - 1) = 34$ (not a perfect square).\n- For $(a, r) = (3, 12)$: $a(r - 1) = 3(12 - 1) = 33$ (not a perfect square).\n- For $(a, r) = (4, 9)$: $a(r - 1) = 4(9 - 1) = 32$ (not a perfect square).\n- For $(a, r) = (6, 6)$: $a(r - 1) = 6(6 - 1) = 30$ (not a perfect square).\n- For $(a, r) = (9, 4)$: $a(r - 1) = 9(4 - 1) = 27$ (not a perfect square).\n- For $(a, r) = (12, 3)$: $a(r - 1) = 12(3 - 1) = 24$ (not a perfect square).\n- For $(a, r) = (18, 2)$: $a(r - 1) = 18(2 - 1) = 18$ (not a perfect square).\n- For $(a, r) = (36, 1)$: $a(r - 1) = 36(1 - 1) = 0$ (perfect square).\n\nThus, the valid pair is $(a, r) = (18, 2)$:\n\\[ a = 18, \\quad r = 2. \\]\n\nNow, calculate $b$ and $c$:\n\\[ b = ar = 18 \\times 2 = 36, \\]\n\\[ c = ar^2 = 18 \\times 2^2 = 72. \\]\n\nFinally, sum $a$, $b$, and $c$:\n\\[ a + b + c = 18 + 36 + 72 = 126. \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{126}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{126}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
